  {
    "id": "95ad8ad0-a922-4493-85a2-f9917f626045",
    "question": "Found and wired an orphaned Subscription.scheduleDowngrade() domain method into a real customer self-service action",
    "answer": "Same class of gap as NotificationPreference/SocialLead this session: Subscription.scheduleDowngrade(planId) was a fully-implemented, tested-shape domain method with a real invariant guard (only active/paused subs), but zero API route ever called it -- pending_downgrade_plan_id sat on subscription_master forever null. Wired it into PATCH /api/customer/subscription as a new self-service action, validates the target plan exists/is active, rejects choosing the current plan, persists pending_downgrade_plan_id. Extended GET /api/plans to also return each plan's id (previously only slug) so the customer UI could reference a real plan id. New UI on /customer/subscription: a plan-switch dropdown + a banner showing an already-scheduled downgrade. Verified live: domain-class test confirms correct state transition and the cancelled-subscription guard rejects it, confirmed the exact DB write and both plan-validation query outcomes (found/not-found), confirmed 401 on GET+PATCH, cleaned up test data, tsc clean. Marked status=PARTIAL, not real -- there is no immediate-upgrade action (only scheduled downgrade) and no billing/renewal cron job anywhere in this codebase to actually apply the pending downgrade at the next cycle (no payment gateway wired, so there's no real renewal event to hook into yet). Registry now: 353 real / 539 not_built / 40 partial / 7 n_a / 1 blocked.",
